# Dart & Flutter Standards

## Widgets

- Prefer `const` constructors everywhere possible.
- Use `StatelessWidget` unless local mutable state is strictly needed.
- Extract sub-widgets into separate classes instead of deeply nesting `build()` methods.
- Always pass `key: super.key` in widget constructors.

```dart
// ✅ GOOD
class CustomerCard extends StatelessWidget {
  const CustomerCard({super.key, required this.customer});
  final Customer customer;
  ...
}
```

## Async / Error Handling

Never swallow exceptions silently.

```dart
// ❌ BAD
try { await api.fetchCustomers(); } catch (_) {}

// ✅ GOOD
try {
  await api.fetchCustomers();
} catch (e, stack) {
  Sentry.captureException(e, stackTrace: stack);
  rethrow;
}
```

## Strings & Localisation

- All user-visible strings must come from `AppLocalizations.of(context)!`.
- Never hardcode Italian or English text in widgets.

## Logging

- Never use `print()` — use `debugPrint()` in debug builds or `Sentry.captureMessage` for production-relevant events.

## Imports

- Order: dart: → package: → relative.
- Use relative imports within `lib/` (`import '../widgets/foo.dart'`), not `package:powercoach_studio/...`.

## Naming

| Concept | Convention |
|---------|-----------|
| Files | `snake_case.dart` |
| Classes / Types | `PascalCase` |
| Variables / functions | `camelCase` |
| Constants | `camelCase` (Dart style) or `SCREAMING_SNAKE` for enums |

## Codegen (Drift / l10n)

After modifying Drift tables or ARB files run:

```bash
dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs
```
